Use fresh storage for each test, accept consent through the UI, and reload to verify persistence.

The selectors below assume a dialog named **Cookie consent** and an **Accept all** button. The one application-specific synchronization detail is the CMP's initialization signal: this example uses an app-owned `data-consent-ready="true"` attribute on `<html>`. Your CMP integration should set it only after reading stored consent and resolving banner rendering. Replace the helper with your actual CMP readiness signal; the test must not set the signal itself.

```ts
// tests/consent.spec.ts
import { test, expect, type Page } from '@playwright/test';

test.use({
  // Explicitly override any saved authentication/consent state.
  storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] },
});

const banner = (page: Page) =>
  page.getByRole('dialog', { name: 'Cookie consent', exact: true });

async function waitForConsentReady(page: Page) {
  await expect(page.locator('html')).toHaveAttribute(
    'data-consent-ready',
    'true',
    { timeout: 10_000 },
  );
}

test('first visit shows the consent banner', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/');
  await waitForConsentReady(page);
  await expect(banner(page)).toBeVisible();
});

test('accepted consent suppresses the banner after reload', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/');
  await waitForConsentReady(page);

  await expect(banner(page)).toBeVisible();
  await banner(page)
    .getByRole('button', { name: 'Accept all', exact: true })
    .click();
  await expect(banner(page)).toBeHidden();

  // Preserve this context's cookies/localStorage for the returning visit.
  await page.reload();
  await waitForConsentReady(page);
  await expect(banner(page)).toBeHidden();
});
```

Set `use.baseURL` to your test site in `playwright.config.ts`, then run:

```bash
npx playwright test tests/consent.spec.ts
```

Each test owns a separate fresh context, so the second test establishes its own accepted state instead of depending on the first. Explicit empty storage also prevents inherited saved consent from invalidating the first-visit scenario. Playwright supports per-test context configuration and storage state overrides. genui{"citation":{"ref":"turn19view1"}}

Waiting for consent initialization after reload matters: an immediate absence assertion can pass while an asynchronously loaded banner has not yet appeared. Run against a test environment configured to display the first-visit experience.

To verify the test's sensitivity, temporarily break consent persistence in a test build and confirm the reload assertion fails, then restore it. I have not executed these tests against your application. This covers banner behavior and persistence; cookie and tracking-request checks would be separate tests.

Here is a Pact.js v16 consumer test using Node's test runner. It assumes `id` is a JSON number; if your API returns `"123"`, change the matcher and client type expectations to strings.

Install Pact:

```bash
npm install --save-dev @pact-foundation/pact@^16
```

Exercise the application's actual API client. If you already have one, import that instead of adding this minimal client:

```js
// src/user-api.mjs
export async function getUser(baseURL, id) {
  const response = await fetch(
    new URL(`/api/users/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`, baseURL),
    { headers: { Accept: 'application/json' } },
  );
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`GET user failed: ${response.status}`);
  }
  return response.json();
}
```

```js
// tests/user-api.pact.test.mjs
import { test } from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import path from 'node:path';
import {
  Pact,
  Matchers,
  SpecificationVersion,
} from '@pact-foundation/pact';
import { getUser } from '../src/user-api.mjs';

test('GET /api/users/123 supplies the fields the consumer needs', async () => {
  const example = {
    id: 123,
    name: 'Ada Lovelace',
    email: 'ada@example.test',
  };

  const pact = new Pact({
    consumer: 'web-consumer',
    provider: 'users-api',
    dir: path.resolve('pacts'),
    spec: SpecificationVersion.SPECIFICATION_VERSION_V4,
    logLevel: 'warn',
  });

  await pact
    .addInteraction()
    .given('user 123 exists')
    .uponReceiving('a request for user 123')
    .withRequest('GET', '/api/users/123', builder => {
      builder.headers({ Accept: 'application/json' });
    })
    .willRespondWith(200, builder => {
      builder.headers({ 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
      builder.jsonBody({
        id: Matchers.integer(example.id),
        name: Matchers.string(example.name),
        email: Matchers.string(example.email),
      });
    })
    .executeTest(async mockServer => {
      const user = await getUser(mockServer.url, 123);
      assert.deepEqual(user, example);
    });
});
```

Run:

```bash
node --test tests/user-api.pact.test.mjs
```

A successful execution verifies the request made by the consumer and writes `pacts/web-consumer-users-api.json`. The matchers require an integer and two strings while permitting different provider values. The exact assertion checks the consumer's handling of the mock's known example; it does not change the provider's type matchers into exact-value requirements. This follows Pact's fluent consumer API and matcher model. genui{"citation":{"refs":["turn16view0","turn21view1"]}}

Use a clean pact output directory for each CI run. The provider must implement the `user 123 exists` state by setting up a real test user, then verify this pact against its application. A passing consumer test alone does not prove the deployed provider is compatible. Publishing pacts, provider verification, and a broker `can-i-deploy` gate are the next integration steps; none has been run here.

Configure Vitest's V8 provider with **80% global branch coverage** and **90% branch coverage for payments**. The 90% payments floor is a proposed stricter threshold; adjust it if your team has already chosen another value.

Install the coverage package at the exact version of your installed Vitest:

```bash
VITEST_COVERAGE_VERSION=$(node -p "require('vitest/package.json').version")
npm install --save-dev --save-exact "@vitest/coverage-v8@${VITEST_COVERAGE_VERSION}"
```

Merge this into your existing configuration, preserving its plugins, aliases, and test settings:

```ts
// vitest.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';

export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    coverage: {
      provider: 'v8',
      reportsDirectory: './coverage',
      reporter: ['text', 'html', 'lcov', 'json', 'json-summary'],
      reportOnFailure: true,

      // Include application files even when no test imports them.
      include: ['src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}'],
      exclude: [
        '**/*.d.ts',
        '**/*.{test,spec}.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}',
        '**/__tests__/**',
      ],

      thresholds: {
        perFile: false,
        branches: 80,
        'src/payments/**': {
          branches: 90,
        },
      },
    },
  },
});
```

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "test:coverage": "vitest run --coverage"
  }
}
```

**What this enforces:** the complete included source set must reach 80% branches, and the aggregate of files matching `src/payments/**` must reach 90%. Payments still contributes to the global total. These are module-level thresholds; add `perFile: true` inside the payments block only if you want every payments file individually to meet 90%. Vitest documents both glob thresholds and their contribution to the global total. genui{"citation":{"ref":"turn16view1"}}

Replace the payments glob with your actual directory. Check that payment files, including unimported ones, appear in the report; a misspelled glob can leave the intended module outside its stricter gate. The exclusion list is deliberately limited to test and declaration files. Add generated-only exclusions when applicable, with a written rationale.

