Use three isolated checkout attempts, with a new order and PaymentIntent for every test and retry. These are Stripe's documented test cards: success `4242424242424242`, generic decline `4000000000000002`, and insufficient funds `4000000000009995`. Both failures have error code `card_declined`; their decline codes differ. [Stripe test cards](https://docs.stripe.com/testing).

This template assumes an embedded Payment Element. Your app-specific `checkout` fixture must create an unpaid sandbox order, return its URL, PaymentIntent ID and publishable key, provide `readOrder()` returning `{ paymentStatus, fulfilled }`, and clean up its own data. Those fixtures and the UI names below need mapping to your application.

```ts
// e2e/checkout.spec.ts
import Stripe from 'stripe';
import { test, expect } from './fixtures/checkout';

const secret = process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY;
if (!secret?.startsWith('sk_test_')) {
  throw new Error('STRIPE_SECRET_KEY must be a Stripe test key');
}
const stripe = new Stripe(secret);

const cases = [
  { name: 'Visa succeeds', pan: '4242424242424242', decline: null },
  { name: 'generic decline', pan: '4000000000000002', decline: 'generic_decline' },
  { name: 'insufficient funds', pan: '4000000000009995', decline: 'insufficient_funds' },
] as const;

for (const scenario of cases) {
  test(scenario.name, async ({ page, checkout }) => {
    expect(checkout.publishableKey).toMatch(/^pk_test_/);
    const initial = await stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(checkout.paymentIntentId);
    expect(initial.livemode).toBe(false);
    expect(initial.status).toBe('requires_payment_method');
    expect(await checkout.readOrder()).toMatchObject({
      paymentStatus: 'pending', fulfilled: false,
    });

    await page.goto(checkout.url);
    // App-owned wrapper must contain the single card-entry iframe.
    const card = page.getByTestId('stripe-payment-element').frameLocator('iframe');
    await card.getByPlaceholder('Card number').fill(scenario.pan);
    const year = String(new Date().getUTCFullYear() + 2).slice(-2);
    await card.getByPlaceholder('MM / YY').fill(`12 / ${year}`);
    await card.getByPlaceholder('CVC').fill('123');
    await card.getByPlaceholder('ZIP').fill('42424');
    await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Pay', exact: true }).click();

    if (scenario.decline === null) {
      await expect(page).toHaveURL(/\/success(?:[/?#]|$)/);
      await expect(page.getByText('Payment succeeded', { exact: true })).toBeVisible();
      await expect.poll(async () =>
        (await stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(checkout.paymentIntentId)).status,
        { timeout: 30_000 },
      ).toBe('succeeded');
      // Read persisted app state after its verified webhook has completed.
      await expect.poll(() => checkout.readOrder(), { timeout: 30_000 })
        .toMatchObject({ paymentStatus: 'paid', fulfilled: true });
    } else {
      const message = scenario.decline === 'insufficient_funds'
        ? /insufficient funds/i : /your card was declined/i;
      await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toHaveText(message);
      await expect.poll(async () => {
        const intent = await stripe.paymentIntents.retrieve(checkout.paymentIntentId);
        return {
          status: intent.status,
          code: intent.last_payment_error?.code,
          decline: intent.last_payment_error?.decline_code,
        };
      }, { timeout: 30_000 }).toEqual({
        status: 'requires_payment_method', code: 'card_declined',
        decline: scenario.decline,
      });
      await expect(page).not.toHaveURL(/\/success(?:[/?#]|$)/);
      expect(await checkout.readOrder()).toMatchObject({
        paymentStatus: 'pending', fulfilled: false,
      });
    }
  });
}
```

The `frameLocator` handles cross-origin inputs; it must target the actual card-entry frame, not an arbitrary frame index. For Stripe-hosted Checkout, adapt the page/frame entry point. Keep the secret key in the Node test process. These tests check UI plus authoritative payment state; proving fulfillment only follows a verified webhook also needs a separate delayed/forged-webhook test. This draft has not run against your checkout.

This starts at **20 VUs**, reaches **100 after 5 minutes**, holds **100 for 10 minutes**, and ramps to zero over **1 minute**. I chose one minute for ramp-down because you did not specify its duration. The scheduled workload is 16 minutes, with a small grace period for in-flight requests.

```js
// load-tests/ramp.js
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

const baseURL = __ENV.BASE_URL;
const path = __ENV.TARGET_PATH || '/api/products';
if (!baseURL || !/^https?:\/\//.test(baseURL)) {
  throw new Error('Set BASE_URL to the authorized load-test environment');
}
if (!path.startsWith('/')) throw new Error('TARGET_PATH must start with /');

// This example endpoint's contract is HTTP 200, including under load.
http.setResponseCallback(http.expectedStatuses(200));

export const options = {
  discardResponseBodies: true,
  scenarios: {
    load: {
      executor: 'ramping-vus',
      startVUs: 20,
      stages: [
        { duration: '5m', target: 100 },
        { duration: '10m', target: 100 },
        { duration: '1m', target: 0 },
      ],
      gracefulRampDown: '10s',
      gracefulStop: '10s',
    },
  },
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(95)<500'],
    http_req_failed: ['rate<0.01'],
  },
};

export default function () {
  const response = http.get(`${baseURL.replace(/\/$/, '')}${path}`, {
    timeout: '10s',
    redirects: 0,
    tags: { name: 'load-target' },
  });
  check(response, { 'returns 200': result => result.status === 200 });
  sleep(1); // Explicit think time; each VU loops after its request completes.
}
```

Run after setting `BASE_URL` and `TARGET_PATH` for your staging or dedicated load environment:

```bash
k6 run load-tests/ramp.js
```

The thresholds make the process exit nonzero if p95 is **500 ms or more**, or the request failure rate is **1% or more**. The status expectation makes unexpected HTTP responses count as failures; the `check` adds a readable assertion result. Redirects are disabled so an authentication redirect cannot masquerade as a successful API response.

The [ramping-vus executor](https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/using-k6/scenarios/executors/ramping-vus/) uses `startVUs` for the initial population and stage targets for subsequent ramps. VUs model concurrency, not a fixed requests-per-second rate: slower responses lower throughput in this closed workload. The budgets apply to the aggregate run. If you need separate budgets during the 100-VU plateau, tag that phase and add matching threshold filters.

